A rotation creates one place where the next value is smaller than the value before it. The index of that smaller value is the rotation count.

For instance, in `[5, 6, 7, 1, 2, 3, 4]`, the first drop is from `7` to `1`, and `1` is at index `3`. If no drop appears, the array is already sorted, so the result is `0`.

This only reads the array and does not modify it.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int findKRotation(vector<int> &nums) {
        for (int i = 1; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); i++) {
            if (nums[i] < nums[i - 1]) {
                return i;
            }
        }

        return 0;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case, with an early return when the drop is found  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

This version uses the same minimum-element idea, but the search is written recursively. The sorted part tells which half can still contain the rotation point.

For `[3, 4, 5, 1, 2]`, the search ends at index `3`, so three right rotations were used. An empty list returns `0` before the recursive calls begin.

```java
class Solution {
    public int findKRotation(ArrayList<Integer> nums) {
        if (nums == null || nums.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        return findMinimumIndex(nums, 0, nums.size() - 1);
    }

    private int findMinimumIndex(ArrayList<Integer> nums, int left, int right) {
        if (left == right) {
            return left;
        }

        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if (nums.get(middle) > nums.get(right)) {
            return findMinimumIndex(nums, middle + 1, right);
        }

        return findMinimumIndex(nums, left, middle);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`  
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the recursive call stack

I kept this one direct by looking for the smallest value. In a sorted rotated array, the smallest value is where the original sorted array starts again. Its index is the number of rotations.

For example, `[4, 5, 1, 2, 3]` has its minimum at index `2`, so the answer is `2`. An empty array is also handled by returning `0`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int findKRotation(vector<int> &nums) {
        if (nums.empty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        int minimumIndex = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); i++) {
            if (nums[i] < nums[minimumIndex]) {
                minimumIndex = i;
            }
        }

        return minimumIndex;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

The minimum element is the useful point here. Instead of checking every value, the binary search compares the middle value with the rightmost value.

If `nums[mid]` is larger, the minimum must be to the right. Otherwise, the minimum can be at `mid` or to its left. The input is not changed. This assumes the usual problem condition that the sorted values are distinct.

An already sorted array keeps moving `right` left until the answer becomes `0`.

```python
class Solution:
    def findKRotation(self, nums):
        if not nums:
            return 0

        left = 0
        right = len(nums) - 1

        while left < right:
            middle = left + (right - left) // 2

            if nums[middle] > nums[right]:
                left = middle + 1
            else:
                right = middle

        return left
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

## Intuition
A sorted array that has been rotated still contains two sorted portions. The smallest element is the pivot point where the rotation happened. The index of this minimum element directly represents the number of rotations.

Instead of scanning the entire array (which would take O(n)), we can exploit the sorted property and apply binary search to locate this pivot efficiently.

The key observation is that by comparing the middle element with the rightmost element, we can determine which side of the array contains the minimum element. This allows us to discard half of the search space in every iteration.

## Approach
Initialize two pointers:

low = 0

high = n - 1

Perform binary search while low < high.

Compute the middle index:

mid = (low + high) / 2

Compare nums[mid] with nums[high]:

If nums[mid] > nums[high]

The minimum element lies in the right half of the array.

Move the search space to the right by setting low = mid + 1.

Otherwise

The minimum element lies in the left half or at mid.

Narrow the search space by setting high = mid.

Continue shrinking the search space until low == high.

At this point, both pointers indicate the index of the minimum element, which is also the number of rotations.

## Solution
```cpp[]

class Solution {
public:
    int findKRotation(vector<int> &nums)  {
        int n=nums.size();
       int low=0;
       int high=n-1;

       while(low<high){
        int mid=(low+high)/2;
        if(nums[mid]>nums[high]){

            low=mid+1;
        }
        else{

            high=mid;
        }
       }

        return low;
    }
};
```

